00:00:02.630 --> 00:00:08.549 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  my name is derek wengee i am the  director of sneaker operations at the  cobblers in miami florida  today we're going to be soul swapping a  original 1985 pair of  jordan chicago ones with the donor sold  and these actually released in 1985 with  a retail of just 65  for reference the same pair in 1985 for  65  is now in the aftermarket brand new for  about twenty thousand dollars and that's  if you can even find a pair  [Music]  the first step that we need to do is  acquire a donor shoe in this case the  client had provided a donor shoe which  is actually the 2015 retro which is  merely just a re-release of the shoe  on this particular pair the customer  requested that we leave the upper of the  shoe which is all of the leather  he did not want it cleaned he did not  want it painted he didn't want any  conditioners  so we need to carefully take off the  sole without you know damaging the sole  or altering anything in this case the  upper of the donor shoe  is not as important but sometimes we'll  use those parts for other things  so what i like to start with is just  using heat and acetone ideally i'll pour  the acetone inside of the shoe to  actually loosen up the glue  that is on the footbed and the side  walls of the shoe to kind of break that  down to give me an entry point in the  side  once we're able to get to that layer of  stitching on the side walls we can then  use  an exacto knife as you see i'm using  here or you can also use a seam ripper  to undo those stitches  once the the side wall and stitching is  undone it should be pretty easy from  there we can either just pull the upper  apart or continue using heat or the  acetone to break up that glue  to separate the sole from the upper ear  it's a similar process to remove the  original upper from the sole if it's not  already detached in this case there is  stitching here as you can see  however we don't want to use excess heat  and we also definitely don't want to  pour acetone into the shoe as that would  damage the upper of the original  on the sides here you'll see that i use  a bone folder which is just  bone from an animal it's just a not a  sharp edge but a blunt enough edge that  i can open up the side walls to get down  into that stitching there  once the two pieces are completely  separated we are going to remove  all of the dust and crumbling inside of  the original shoe as well as from the  upper to prepare this  upper of the original shoe to be put on  the donor shoe  [Music]  we use a contact cement it is made for  shoes it is flexible it's clear so it's  perfect for what we're doing here  and it's very important with any type of  restoration we do when we're working  with glue especially in this case  that we get a clean surface and get that  fully prepped and all of the old glue  any dust or any buildup oil water that  may have seeped into that shoe  the reasoning for this is because if we  don't have a clean surface for the glue  to adhere to the glue is not going to  fully do its job in holding that shoe  together for the longest period of time  we will apply one thin layer to the  upper in this case as well as on  the sole of the donor shoe and we allow  that to dry  a tip that i like to use is actually to  let this glue over here so it gives me  more  time to fit the sneaker as opposed to  having to put it all together in one  shot  and this again is so we can line up the  upper properly on the sole so there's no  distinction  between the original muscle and the  donor sole being lined up on this 1985.  [Music]  once we've heated up the glue and  pressed it with our hand to make sure  everything is fitting properly  we will then just double check and make  sure everything is lined up the way we'd  like so everything looks original  we'll then take the original last from  the shoe which is  just the interior of the shoe if you can  think of it as what shoe makers use to  make and shape  shoes go place that into the shoe and  then we will take it over to the bladder  press what the bladder press is going to  do is basically give us that extra  security and push the shoe together  and make sure that the glue is fully  bonded and has a strong hold to it  once we're done you can see here that we  actually stitch by hand there is a  machine but with something this delicate  and this  fragile and rare we actually prefer  stitching it by hand just so we know  that every stitch is fit perfectly into  the original hole  to make things look as original as  possible as you can see this isn't a  pure white thread we're using we  actually have an  age or more of a vanilla cream thread  which is going to match the original  look of that shoe  if we were to put a bright brilliant  white lace or thread in there  it would kind of take away from the  originality of keeping the upper a  little distressed  and having the soul looking brand new on  top of an old distressed 1985  shoe we actually add a little bit of wax  here to imitate the original shoe upon  [Music]  release  now that everything's stitched up it may  not be mind-blowing before and after  just putting a sole onto a new shoe but  to us in these little details this makes  the shoe complete and i think the  customer's going to be very happy with  these results  [Music]  [Music]  you

